    "result_text": "I fixed `mock_openai()`, and both digest tests pass again. There's one leftover: `tests/test_zz_chk.py`, a scratch test I wrote to check the fix. My command to delete it was denied (deleting files needs your approval), so please delete it or let me do it.\n\n**Cause:** openai 3.19.2 no longer sends requests through `httpx`. It uses `httpx2`, a separate package with its own `Client`. respx 0.23.1 only patches `httpx` and `httpcore`, so the router was still set up but never saw the SDK's requests. That's why nothing warned you.\n\n**Fix (only `tests/fakes.py` changed):**\n- Inside the `with` block, I replace the method `httpx2.Client._transport_for_url` on the class itself, using `unittest.mock.patch.object`. This is the same method respx patches on `httpx.Client`. Because it's replaced on the class, it covers every sync client: app.digest's cached one, clients created before the block, and clients created inside it. The patch is removed when the block exits.\n- For URLs under `https://api.openai.com/v1/`, the patched method returns an `httpx2.MockTransport`:\n  - `POST /chat/completions` gets the next canned reply, or a 400 once the replies run out, as before.\n  - Any other OpenAI request raises an `AssertionError`, like respx did for unmatched routes.\n  - Requests to other hosts go to the real transport.\n- The respx router is still there and shares the same reply logic, so clients built on the old `httpx` keep working.\n- The helper's interface and yielded values are unchanged, and I didn't touch `app/` or the pytest configuration.\n\n**Verification:**\n- `pytest -q`: both digest tests pass.\n- The scratch test also passed (3 passed in total). With one client created before the block and one inside it, the first got the canned reply, the second got the 400, and both bodies were recorded. After the block, the method was restored, and a request from the earlier client went to the real network, where pytest-socket blocked it. Nothing was recorded.\n\n**Context7:** CLAUDE.md asks for"
  },
